# ExItS-SaaS Permanent Workflow Rules

## Repository Safety

- ExItS is an independent multi-product portfolio repository. Do not recreate, clone, import, or nest a HealthCare (or other external product) source tree into this workspace without explicit authorization.
- Platform integration contracts under `src/Platform/.../Integration/HealthCare/` are tracked Platform files (versioned contract boundaries) — not a product source tree.
- Preserve ownership boundaries: product operational data belongs in the product database; no cross-product database access or foreign keys; POS must not contain PHI; future products require separate authorization.
- Do not implement future work packages or adjacent product capability without explicit authorization.
- Do not implement PinoyBusinessPOS unless explicitly authorized.

## Git Workflow

- Begin with `git status`, branch, upstream (`-vv`), recent log, and `git ls-remote` verification.
- Stop on unexpected divergence or contamination. Never force-push or rewrite history.
- Do not reset, rebase, merge, or amend unless explicitly authorized.
- Before commit: review `git status`, `git diff`, staged diff, and `git diff --check`.
- Use focused conventional commits (e.g. `feat(admin): ...`, `docs(platform): ...`).
- Record feature hashes in the phase page, portfolio progress, and completion report.
- A small documentation hash-record commit is allowed.
- Push only after restore, Release build, tests, and required validation succeed.
- Final local and remote `main` must match; final working tree must be clean.
- Verify portfolio independence before and after material changes: no root `HealthCare/` directory; `git ls-files -- HealthCare/` empty; `dotnet sln ExItS.slnx list` has no HealthCare project.

## Build and Test Workflow

- Restore, Release-build, and test the complete `ExItS.slnx`.
- Record warnings, errors, passed, failed, and skipped totals.
- Never weaken or remove tests to make a work package pass.
- Use PostgreSQL/Testcontainers for relational behavior; do not use EF InMemory as proof of PostgreSQL behavior.
- Validate migration apply, rollback, and re-apply where persistence changes.
- Never target an unknown or production database.
- Do not automatically apply production migrations at API startup (`Migrate()` forbidden on production paths).

## Security and Architecture

- Do not commit credentials, secrets, payment credentials, card data, PHI, or production configuration.
- Preserve Platform/Product database and ownership boundaries; no cross-product DB access or cross-database FKs.
- Domain remains persistence-independent; Application must not reference Infrastructure.
- No generic repositories. API/UI DTOs must not expose EF entities.
- Development-stage unauthenticated APIs must not be described as production-secure.
- Do not invent fake production authentication.
- UI projects must not reference Infrastructure, EF Core, or Npgsql.
- Platform Admin uses native CSS / Razor / Blazor — no Ant Design, no Tailwind.

## Documentation and Reporting

- Update only documents affected by actual work.
- Keep README, FILE-MANIFEST, phase progress, risks, release plan, and completion reports consistent with code.
- Do not claim unimplemented capability.
- Completion reports must include: status; delivered capability; explicit exclusions; persistence/migrations when relevant; API/UI capability; build/test/runtime evidence; security limitations; portfolio-independence evidence; risks/open decisions; files/docs changed; Git/push evidence; exact next work package.
- Historical signed-off reports may retain accurate historical evidence; do not rewrite them merely to erase history unless an active authoritative document is now materially misleading.
